From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Fri, 15 Jul 1994 10:44:02 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-136] emergence defined

 derek@cs.wisc.edu  (Derek Zahn) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-7-129:
>Mm.  So, are chess endgames of this kind emergent or not?  It seems you
>want to say not, since we can look up the answer in some hypothetical
>data structure enumerating the possibilities, a process that is faster
>than "simulation" by searching the game tree (and since I asked you
>for a non-emergent phenomenon).  But what about chess starting from
>move 1?  Just a bigger "book of chess endings", right? Can't I build
>a book like that for weather too?  And for neural nets?

 A book of chess endings is not a full-fledged lookup table. It is a
set of rules reflecting our knowledge of how chess works, enabling us
to classify an unmanageably large number of possible positions into
a small enough set that a human can handle them.
 I would be very surprised if you could do the same for weather or
chess from move 1 in the forseeable future.
