From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Fri, 15 Jul 94 15:40:37 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-137] If Knowledge Limits Learning (was: AI development acceleration)

Robin Hanson:
> Now if Lenat is right that one can just drop the spatial stuff and 
> make do with a much smaller computer than a full "human-equivalent"
> computer, maybe we will have enough hardware to run such a Tabla Rasa
> simulation before we could even run an upload at normal human speed.

My guess is that Lenat is wrong in a number of ways.  First I don't
think you can run some human-logic/language equivalent on a greatly
reduced hardware platform.  Second I think his EURISKO/CYC approach is
a dead end.  I do not believe that intelligence can be reduced to
symbol/logic/language manipulation, in any meaningful way.  Third,
believing that it is more efficient to load knowledge in, rather than
develop knowledge acquistion skills, is just putting off the evil day
when CYC's inability to learn, usefully, becomes painfully obvious.

However that doesn't mean that the converse that Robin presents:

> But if we need near full human equivalence, I would guess we'd have
> uploads long before any such Tabla Rasa is feasible [.....]

is right either.  My guess is the opposite.  Uploads come quite
considerably after, in technological terms, the ultra-intelligent
machines arrive.  Uploads have their own problems.  Like developing the
neurological interface and a complete understanding of the human brain. 
This is going to require full-blown nanotech, IMO, and huge information
processing resources.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

