Mike Price >
"My guess is that Lenat is wrong in a number of ways.  First I don't
think you can run some human-logic/language equivalent on a greatly
reduced hardware platform.  Second I think his EURISKO/CYC approach is
a dead end.  I do not believe that intelligence can be reduced to
symbol/logic/language manipulation, in any meaningful way.  Third,
believing that it is more efficient to load knowledge in, rather than
develop knowledge acquistion skills, is just putting off the evil day
when CYC's inability to learn, usefully, becomes painfully obvious."

I tend to agree with the above.  Intelligence seems to be an emergent
property of dumb hive activities of individual neurons.  Computer AI
simulations try to reverse this.  It seems that this approach is a result of
the logic of past and present computer design (serial processing).  It is
likely that future parallel processing computers will be able to evolve human
like intelligence but the irony will come that we will still not understand
what happened due to the evolved unplanned complexity of the intelligent
system.  

A good book touching on the above and on other interesting topics "Out of
Control-The Rise of Neo-Biological Civilization" by Kevin Kelly 1994.  I'm
new to the list so don't know if this book is a common reference here.


