From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Sat, 16 Jul 94 07:19:42 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-142] If Knowledge Limits Learning 

Me:
> I think [Lenat's] EURISKO/CYC approach is a dead end.  I do not
> believe that intelligence can be reduced to symbol/logic/language
> manipulation, in any meaningful way.

Robin Hanson:
> I don't see how you could possibly know enough to know this. 

I did not say I knew it, I said I believed it, which is a weaker
statement.  Neural nets do not manipulate symbols as their basic
operations.  If our minds operate in some similar - although much more
complex - fashion, as I believe the evidence is increasingly saying,
then neither do we.  I view logic, language and symbol manipulation as
mere epiphenomena of neural activity and trying to model human symbol
manipulation directly is a mistake.  IMO.  

> Note that my estimate of your approach needing several hundred
> thousand human-brain-years worth of simulation does not depend on
> symbolic vs. other AI.

I didn't comment directly on your analysis because it didn't seem
applicable to neural nets and I hadn't realised it was intended as such. 
You had said that you assumed:
> .... the number of valuable chunks one discovers per unit of internal
> processing time is proportional to the number of chunks one already
> has .....
A blank, simple net should never learn anything by this logic.  Yet they
do.  So why should I believe that this assumption, plucked from nowhere
as far as I can see, applies to more complex things, and complex neural
nets in particular, when it doesn't apply to simple nets?

Even so you seemed to be making the assumption that the neural nets have
to discover everything for themselves.  Why can't they be trained like
humans?  I see no reason why a human-level neural net running on a
hardware platform of approx 10^17 bits/sec should take any longer than
a 10^17 bits/sec human to learn about the world.  And running on a
faster platform should take even less time.  Note that once one net is
trained to any particular level it can be cheaply copied, just as
uploads could.

> How could uploads possibly require more computation than this?
> 
> And uploads do not require "a complete understanding of the human
> brain", only an ability to reasonably model some low, probably
> neuronal, level.

I concede a complete understanding is not necessary, I was thinking of
a low-level understanding, as well.  But to get the uploadee's data out
you have to dissect their brain down to at least the dendritic/ synaptic
sub-neuronal level, unless you can come up with some fancy,
instantaneous non-invasive, high-resolution deep tissue molecular
scanner.  I note that even glial cells are now suspected of playing a
role in memory/LTP, so tread carefully :-)

> [Is] it obvious that you will find the right local neural net learning
> rule before this?

No, but I'd bet and speculate on it.  In a sense I have bet on it, since
I'm signed for cryonics.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

