From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Sat, 16 Jul 1994 18:08:32 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-7-145] emergence defined

Freeman Craig:

> For chess, the table of known winning endings is already large (Fine,
> _Basic Chess Endings_, is < 600 pages most of which is human readable
> heuristic text). The reason chess is an interesting case is that the
> search space is ridiculously large
...
> The only sense in which " K B B mates K " is emergent is that it
> follows from the rules of chess and the will of the stronger side to
> win.

Yes, Shannon or somebody guessed that chess's game tree has something
like 10^300 nodes.  Chess is one of those things that only computer
scientists can call "computable" and maintain a straight face.
My purpose in wondering about a lookup table for chess from the
start position was merely to point out that a definition of emergence
based on difficulty of modeling has to measure memory capacity as
part of what is meant by "difficult".

Chess, though, is very interesting in that a pearl like "K B B mates K"
is *exactly* the kind of thing that typifies emergent phenomena.  It's
a succinct, interesting, recognizable kind of constructed entity
that arises from a system with simple interactions.  The classic
representation of the Mandelbrot Set is emergent in this same way --
and so, I'd claim, is a tornado and the 12-tone scale and winged
flight and black holes and Freeman Craig Presson.

derek
